  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 AM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: Low amplitude longwave troughing begins to develop \nacross Canada as yesterday's shortwave ridge over the Great Lakes \nRegion continues to break down and progress down stream.\nUpstream shortwave currently over the southwestern U.S begins \nto develop surface low pressure and push moisture into the \nMidwest through Thursday. Beneficial rounds of rainfall will \nremain ongoing through Thursday afternoon before this slow \nmoving frontal boundary sweeps to the southeast.\n\nThe remainder of the forecast period is expected to remain pretty \nbenign as the aforementioned longwave trough over Canada expands \nsouthward and supports a cool continental polar air mass to occupy \nthe Great Lakes Region this weekend through the remainder of the \nforecast period. Surface high pressure over northern Ontario will \nkeep things dry and mostly rain-free, besides slight chances of \nrainfall driven by a disheveled wave pivoting across the \nU.S./Canadian Border this Sunday. \n\nForecast Details:  \n\nToday and Thursday:  While areas upstream are expected to observe \ninches of rainfall, the warm conveyor belt of moisture from the gulf \nstream appears to remain over the Illinois/Iowa region. Latest hi-\nres guidance continues to support the strongest 850mb warm air \nadvection upstream while forcing falls rapidly with distance as it \napproaches our CWA leading to less intense rainfall. Regardless, \nadequate moisture is still expected to produce some beneficial \nshower to the northwards in two rounds (today and Thursday). Light \nstratiform rainfall will persist today only producing a couple \ntenths followed by a more convective-driven element sweeping the \nregion Thursday resulting in rainfall totals between a quarter to \nhalf inch for most locations.\n\nFriday and Saturday: Post frontal cold air advection builds this \nFriday, resulting in fall-like weather with breezy northwest flow \nand temperatures beginning to fall back to climo-norms. Highs remain \nin the 60s this Friday and Saturday accompanied by surface high \npressure quickly drying out profiles. The only impactful weather \nwithin this timeframe is the near-freezing overnight temperatures \nFriday night. Breezy northwest flow combined with clear skies will \nmaximize radiational cooling rates leading to widespread lows around \nthe 30s for interior northern lower and eastern upper. \n\nSunday through Tuesday Outlook: Overall quiet and non-impactful \nweather is expected to continue with prolonged longwave troughing \nand cP influence through early next week. Only chance of rainfall \nremains on Sunday as a disheveled wave pivots across the \nU.S./Canadian Border. Rainfall amounts remain low with most moisture \nremaining north of the bridge, generally a tenth or so of total \nadditional precip. Otherwise the majority of the weekend through \nearly next week will produce partly/mostly cloudy skies with highs \nin the 60s for most areas.",
